1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:03,840
A kingdom once guarded by
an army of women warriors.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:07,330
This is Benin, known as
the birthplace of Vodun.

3
00:00:08,076 --> 00:00:09,466
The capital is Porto-Novo.

4
00:00:11,202 --> 00:00:16,542
About 14.8 million people live
here, on 114,763 square kilometers.

5
00:00:18,284 --> 00:00:19,714
They speak Fon and Yoruba.

6
00:00:20,184 --> 00:00:24,954
Hello is "A fon gandji" in
Fon, and "Ẹ ǹlẹ́ o" in Yoruba.

7
00:00:26,686 --> 00:00:29,046
And they pay in West African CFA francs.

8
00:00:32,030 --> 00:00:33,230
Some fun facts.

9
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:37,870
And stay until the end, for
a mystery nobody ever solved.

10
00:00:39,447 --> 00:00:41,087
Benin has two capitals.

11
00:00:41,597 --> 00:00:45,287
Porto-Novo is the official one,
but the government sits in Cotonou.

12
00:00:45,922 --> 00:00:48,482
Every January 10, the
whole country takes a

13
00:00:48,532 --> 00:00:51,032
day off for Vodun Day, a national holiday.

14
00:00:51,666 --> 00:00:53,526
Half the people here are under 18.

15
00:00:54,046 --> 00:00:55,756
It is one of the youngest
countries on Earth.

16
00:00:56,767 --> 00:00:58,957
Now, a few surprising places.

17
00:01:00,547 --> 00:01:03,365
Ganvié. A town of about
30,000 people, built

18
00:01:03,415 --> 00:01:05,907
on stilts in the middle of Lake Nokoué.

19
00:01:06,544 --> 00:01:08,524
The Temple of Pythons, in Ouidah.

20
00:01:09,114 --> 00:01:11,904
Dozens of royal pythons live
here, and you can hold one.

21
00:01:12,542 --> 00:01:14,112
The Royal Palaces of Abomey.

22
00:01:14,972 --> 00:01:17,056
Twelve kings ruled from these earthen

23
00:01:17,106 --> 00:01:19,362
walls, now a UNESCO World Heritage site.

24
00:01:20,005 --> 00:01:21,375
Pendjari National Park.

25
00:01:22,025 --> 00:01:24,200
One of the last places
in West Africa where

26
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,425
you can still see wild
lions and elephants.

27
00:01:27,463 --> 00:01:30,353
So, what does $10 get you here?

28
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:35,670
A ride on a zémidjan, the
motorbike taxi: about 50 cents.

29
00:01:36,281 --> 00:01:37,951
A local beer: about $1.

30
00:01:38,588 --> 00:01:41,208
A full plate at a simple
restaurant: about $4.

31
00:01:41,819 --> 00:01:43,969
A taxi across town: about $4.

32
00:01:44,569 --> 00:01:46,959
But a cappuccino costs about $5.

33
00:01:47,409 --> 00:01:49,389
More than at home, and
more than your whole meal.

34
00:01:49,999 --> 00:01:53,283
With $10, you ride, you
eat, you drink, you cross

35
00:01:53,333 --> 00:01:56,209
the city, and you still
have 50 cents left.

36
00:01:57,197 --> 00:01:58,747
Now, the don't list.

37
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:03,470
Don't take photos of government
buildings, military sites or the airport.

38
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:04,610
It is against the law.

39
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:07,550
Don't swim in the ocean
without asking locals first.

40
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:10,210
The currents are very strong,
and people drown every year.

41
00:02:10,849 --> 00:02:11,979
Don't count on your credit card.

42
00:02:12,279 --> 00:02:13,299
Benin runs on cash.

43
00:02:13,944 --> 00:02:17,504
Don't photograph people or Vodun
ceremonies without asking permission first.

44
00:02:18,137 --> 00:02:19,537
Don't hurt a python in Ouidah.

45
00:02:19,887 --> 00:02:20,457
They are sacred.

46
00:02:20,877 --> 00:02:23,837
If one slides into a house, people
gently carry it back to the temple.

47
00:02:24,863 --> 00:02:25,803
Let's talk about culture.

48
00:02:27,447 --> 00:02:30,252
Amiwo. A red corn dough cooked with tomato

49
00:02:30,302 --> 00:02:33,107
and palm oil, usually served with chicken.

50
00:02:33,746 --> 00:02:36,832
The pagne. A bright
wax-print cloth, wrapped

51
00:02:36,882 --> 00:02:39,256
or tailored into everyday clothes.

52
00:02:39,888 --> 00:02:40,798
The talking drum.

53
00:02:41,548 --> 00:02:45,788
Squeeze its cords, and the pitch
rises and falls like a human voice.

54
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:47,830
Time for a little history.

55
00:02:49,447 --> 00:02:54,857
1960. The country wins independence
from France, under the name Dahomey.

56
00:02:55,498 --> 00:02:59,560
1975. Mathieu Kérékou
renames it the People's

57
00:02:59,610 --> 00:03:02,758
Republic of Benin, a Marxist state.

58
00:03:03,399 --> 00:03:06,574
1990. A national conference ends one-party

59
00:03:06,624 --> 00:03:09,339
rule, and Benin becomes a democracy.

60
00:03:10,333 --> 00:03:15,083
And now, the chapter you were all
waiting for: legends and mysteries.

61
00:03:16,713 --> 00:03:18,033
The legend of Ganvié.

62
00:03:18,873 --> 00:03:23,033
Three centuries ago, the Tofinu
people were fleeing slave raiders.

63
00:03:23,503 --> 00:03:28,073
Their king, they say, turned into a
hawk to find a safe island on the lake.

64
00:03:28,513 --> 00:03:32,933
Then he became a giant crocodile, and
carried his people across the water.

65
00:03:33,363 --> 00:03:37,413
The raiders' beliefs forbade them to
fight on water, so the town survived.

66
00:03:38,047 --> 00:03:39,877
The Tree of Forgetfulness.

67
00:03:40,307 --> 00:03:43,617
In Ouidah, captives were
marched toward the slave ships.

68
00:03:43,967 --> 00:03:46,978
On the way, the story
goes, men were made to

69
00:03:47,028 --> 00:03:50,317
circle a tree nine times,
and women seven times.

70
00:03:50,697 --> 00:03:52,657
It was meant to erase
their memories of home.

71
00:03:53,296 --> 00:03:55,566
And the mystery: the Zangbeto.

72
00:03:56,226 --> 00:03:59,876
These spinning haystacks are the
night guardians of the Vodun faith.

73
00:04:00,326 --> 00:04:03,956
Followers say no human
is inside, only a spirit.

74
00:04:04,436 --> 00:04:07,766
During ceremonies, the costume
is lifted to show it empty.

75
00:04:08,296 --> 00:04:09,666
Believers call it proof.

76
00:04:10,136 --> 00:04:12,986
How it is done, no
outsider has ever shown.

77
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:16,550
That was Benin.

78
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:18,640
Which country should we visit next?

79
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:19,970
Tell me in the comments.
